    "evidence_text": "BACKGROUND Diabetes mellitus is a major risk factor for adverse outcomes after acute coronary syndromes (ACS).\n\nTherefore, the use of platelet GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors should be strongly considered in diabetic patients with ACS.\n\nA new look at the heart in diabetes mellitus: from ailing to failing\n\nThis activation in conjunction with glycaemic control is important in determining macrovascular mortality.\n\nCardiac sympathetic dysfunction is partially restored to normal with near normalisation of glycaemia.\n\nInterpretations.\n\nMAIN OUTCOME MEASURES Rates of cardiovascular death, myocardial infarction, and stroke.   \n\n\nThere was no heterogeneity between patients with or without ongoing insulin treatment.\n\nBACKGROUND Patients with metabolic syndrome are at increased risk for cardiovascular complications.\n\nCONCLUSIONS Patients with metabolic syndrome presenting for PCI are at increased risk for subsequent cardiovascular events.\n\nMetabolic syndrome as a risk factor for diabetes.\n\nSome fibrates have reduced or delayed incident diabetes.\n\nExtended-release niacin has a neutral effect and statins are controversial.\n\nACE inhibitors and ARBs are the antihypertensive agents least associated with incident diabetes.\n\nPlatelet activation in type 2 diabetes mellitus.\n\nA variety of mechanisms may be responsible for enhanced platelet aggregation.",
